examples.question_text:
Is $f(x)=(x+1)^2$ one-to-one?
Justify using the horizontal line test.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.6cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-3.5,0)--(1.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.4)--(0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->] plot[domain=-3.2:1.2,samples=80] (\x,{(\x+1)^2});
\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The horizontal line $y=1$ meets the graph at $(-2,1)$ and $(0,1)$.
Two different inputs give the same output.
The graph fails the horizontal line test, so the function is not one-to-one.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.6cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-3.5,0)--(1.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.4)--(0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->] plot[domain=-3.2:1.2,samples=80] (\x,{(\x+1)^2});
\draw[black,dashed] (-3.2,1)--(1.2,1);
\node[left] at (-3.2,1) {$y=1$};
\fill[black] (-2,1) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[black] (0,1) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above left] at (-2,1) {$(-2,1)$};
\node[above right] at (0,1) {$(0,1)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
Is $f(x)=\frac{3}{x-1}$ one-to-one on $(-\infty,1)\cup(1,\infty)$?
Justify using the horizontal line test.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.7cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black,->] (-3.3,0)--(5.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-4.2)--(0,4.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,dashed] (1,-4)--(1,4);
\node[above right] at (1,4) {$x=1$};
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,2,3,4,5} {\draw[black] (\x,-0.07)--(\x,0.07); \node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-4,-2,2,4} {\draw[black] (-0.07,\y)--(0.07,\y); \node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[black,thick,<->] plot[domain=-3:0.2,samples=80] (\x,{3/(\x-1)});
\draw[black,thick,<->] plot[domain=1.8:5,samples=80] (\x,{3/(\x-1)});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Each positive horizontal line meets the right branch once; each negative horizontal line meets the left branch once.
The line $y=0$ meets neither branch.
Every horizontal line meets the graph at most once, so the function is one-to-one.
For example, $y=2$ meets only the right branch, at $\left(\frac{5}{2},2\right)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.7cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{testBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-3.3,0)--(5.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-4.2)--(0,4.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,dashed] (1,-4)--(1,4);
\node[above right] at (1,4) {$x=1$};
\draw[black,thick,<->] plot[domain=-3:0.2,samples=80] (\x,{3/(\x-1)});
\draw[black,thick,<->] plot[domain=1.8:5,samples=80] (\x,{3/(\x-1)});
\draw[testBlue,dashed] (-3,2)--(5,2);
\node[above,text=testBlue] at (4.5,2) {$y=2$};
\fill[black] (2.5,2) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above right] at (2.5,2) {$\left(\frac{5}{2},2\right)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
Is $f(x)=x^3+2$ one-to-one?
Justify using the horizontal line test.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.2cm,y=0.4cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-2,0)--(2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->] plot[domain=-1.6:1.6,samples=100] (\x,{(\x)^3+2});
\draw[black] (-0.06,2)--(0.06,2);
\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,2) {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The graph is strictly increasing over all real inputs. Every horizontal line meets it at most once, so the function is one-to-one.
For example, $y=3$ meets the graph only at $(1,3)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.2cm,y=0.4cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-2,0)--(2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->] plot[domain=-1.6:1.6,samples=100] (\x,{(\x)^3+2});
\draw[black,dashed] (-1.8,3)--(1.8,3);
\node[left] at (-1.8,3) {$y=3$};
\fill[black] (1,3) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below right] at (1,3) {$(1,3)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Complete the square.
$\begin{aligned}f(x)&=-2(x^2-4x)+1\\f(x)&=-2\big((x-2)^2-4\big)+1\\f(x)&=-2(x-2)^2+9\end{aligned}$
The turning point is $(2,9)$ and the parabola opens downwards.
(a) The graph is strictly increasing on $(-\infty,2]$ and strictly decreasing on $[2,\infty)$. Each branch passes the horizontal line test.
(b) Since $5>2$, choose the decreasing branch. The largest such interval is $[2,\infty)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.35cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{branchBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-0.7,0)--(4.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-4)--(0,10.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,<-] plot[domain=-0.5:2,samples=60] (\x,{-2*(\x-2)^2+9});
\draw[branchBlue,thick,->] plot[domain=2:4.5,samples=60] (\x,{-2*(\x-2)^2+9});
\draw[black,dashed] (2,0)--(2,9);
\fill[black] (2,9) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above] at (2,9) {$(2,9)$};
\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (2,0) {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
